
 StoneBranch triggers the daily run (schedule, retry-on-failure, alerting) by kicking off the EMR cluster/step-sequence; EMR Steps handle the sequencing within that one run. That's a very standard split and matches everything we already know about this pipeline.

EMR cluster/job run
  Step 1: fileparser  (on-prem copy → curated_app_fact → S3)
  Step 2: aggregator   (AVV/DVS, velocity rollup, segment-share → base matrix in Redshift)
  Step 3: dq_postagg   (reads base matrix, runs the 13 checks, writes verdict)


  Each step is its own JVM/Spark session — no shared state between them except what's persisted to S3/Redshift in between

  EMR's per-step ActionOnFailure setting: if dq_postagg exits non-zero, EMR can be configured to halt the cluster (TERMINATE_CLUSTER) or cancel remaining steps (CANCEL_AND_WAIT)


python/src/
    fileparser/          <- Stage 1, exists
        __init__.py
        config_parser.py
        curated_app_fact.py
        driver.py
    aggregator/           <- Stage 2, not yet built (placeholder name)
        __init__.py
        driver.py
        ...
    dq_postagg/           <- Stage 3, this is what we're designing
        __init__.py
        config_parser.py
        redshift_reader.py
        checks/
            __init__.py
            registry.py           <- @register_rule_check(rule_id=N) decorator + lookup
            completeness.py
            correctness.py
            reliability.py
            coverage_lineage.py
            rule1_checks.py
            rule2_checks.py
            rule3_checks.py
        results_writer.py
        driver.py
